"""Figure captions for build_explainer.py: key -> (alt text, caption HTML)."""
CAPTIONS = {'F2': ('Per-slot probabilities for one real block', '<b>Figure 1 &mdash; the marginal is flat exactly where the conditional is peaked.</b> One real block (GSM8K frozen-train item 9, block 3, the sentence "This means her overtime rate is $18 + ($18/2) = ..."). For each of the 32 slots: the pale bar is the stock model\'s pass-1 probability of the drafter\'s token with the block still empty (the marginal); the solid bar is the trained judge\'s pass-1 top probability with the block draft-filled (the conditional); the short dash is the stock model\'s own best guess. Green means the drafter\'s token was right, orange wrong. Look at slots 7&ndash;16, where the draft is right at every slot: the marginal averages 0.21 and commits nothing, while the judge averages 0.77 and clears 0.95 at several. Slots 17&ndash;31 are where the drafter went off the rails, and the judge correctly stays unconfident there. This block took 17 passes under the stock rule. <span class="tag t-rep">replay</span>'), 'F4': ('Training curves for round 1 and round 2a', '<b>Figure 2 &mdash; the same data and the same 1,057 steps, with and without the draft flag.</b> Round 1 (orange) never reaches the copy-the-draft baseline of 0.556; round 2a (green) passes it around step 800 and is still rising when the cosine schedule runs out. The two dashed lines in the third panel are the ones that matter: round 2a\'s all-mask accuracy sits exactly on the stock value 0.532 at every evaluation, because gating makes that regime bit-identical to the frozen model, while round 1\'s drifts. The fourth panel shows the real bottleneck &mdash; the judge is precise when confident (triangles near 0.95) but confident at only 39% of slots, and that share was still climbing at the end. <span class="tag t-act">actual</span>'), 'F5': ('Accuracy against cost for every decode variant', '<b>Figure 3 &mdash; the trained judge does not clear the free rule\'s frontier.</b> Each point is a complete decode of the same 100 prompts; bars are the 95% paired bootstrap interval of the accuracy difference from stock. The pale blue line traces the training-free rule as its threshold varies, from stock at the right down to &gamma;=.30. Every green point &mdash; every trained commit rule &mdash; lies on or below that line. The best trained setting lands on top of the free rule at 7.93 passes with one point less accuracy. Note how wide the intervals are: with 100 items, a 1-point and a 4-point deficit are not distinguishable. <span class="tag t-act">actual</span>'), 'F6': ('Precision and commit share against threshold, split by decision type', '<b>Figure 4 &mdash; the judge has two kinds of decision and only one of them is trustworthy.</b> Across 8,687 held-out pass-1 slots, confirmations (green, the judge agrees with the draft) are already at 0.99 precision at &tau;=0.90 and barely improve. Overrides (orange, the judge overrules the draft) start at 0.75 and need &tau;=0.995 to reach the same reliability &mdash; by which point they have shrunk from 9.8% of slots to 2.7%. The right panel converts this into damage: at a single threshold of 0.95 the rule makes 0.46 wrong confident commits per 29-slot block; splitting it into confirm .95 / override .99 cuts that to 0.14 while keeping almost the same number of commits. <span class="tag t-rep">replay</span>'), 'F7': ('Training blocks and drafter agreement per data source', '<b>Figure 5 &mdash; the training corpus, and the bimodality that shapes everything.</b> Left: usable 32-token blocks per source, with grey marking generation still in flight when this was written. Right: the distribution of per-block drafter agreement. The shape is the important part &mdash; agreement is not clustered near its 0.55 mean, it piles up at 0 and at 1. Blocks are either almost entirely drafted right or almost entirely wrong. That is why per-slot acceptance can pay at all, and also why a judge that only confirms cannot help on the blocks that need help most. <span class="tag t-rep">replay</span>'), 'F1': ("The draft-aligned decoding loop with one real block's verdict", '<b>Figure 6 &mdash; the decoding loop, and one real pass-1 verdict.</b> The top row is the loop; the three boxes below are the per-slot verdict. The strip at the bottom is not schematic: it is the round-2a judge\'s actual first-pass decision on GSM8K item 9, block 3. Eight slots commit at once, ten keep the draft token as tentative context, fourteen are re-masked. The tentative slots are the mechanism\'s real contribution &mdash; a stock decoder would have blanked all 24 uncommitted slots and thrown the information away. <span class="tag t-rep">replay</span>')}
